Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets and chamfers: the end of a blend at a vertex where more than three
edges meet (PerformIntersectionAtEnd), past a face that lies between two
faces each tangent to it at the vertex, is cut on those two faces, which
meet along the intersection of their surfaces from the vertex (mitcad#2039,
GitHub mitcad/Mitcad#449).

The case: a low block whose plan is a half disc stands against a wall whose
top is a half cylinder; the wall's flat sides run on tangentially into the
block's curved side and into the wall's curved top. The concave edge where
the block's top meets the wall's front is rounded or chamfered. At each end
of the edge five faces meet: the block's top (F1), the wall's front (F2),
and round the vertex the block's curved side (Face[0]), the wall's flat
side (Face[1]) and the wall's curved top (Face[2]), Edge[1] and Edge[2]
tangent (G1, not G2, so counted as sharp edges). Expected: one valid solid,
the blend's end cut by the block's side and the wall's top, both continued
past the vertex. Observed: an invalid shape 142 mm3 smaller than the body
(a rounding of 1 on a body of 642 mm3), an edge 11 mm in tolerance and the
wall's top without its curves; the chamfer likewise.

The origin: for five edges with Edge[1] tangent and Edge[0] or Edge[2]
tangent ("pro15368"), the end is cut on Face[2]'s surface alone, and
Edge[0] (the edge of F1 and Face[0]), prolonged to CV1, bounds the cut
part of Face[2] up to the vertex. That holds only where Edge[0] lies on
Face[2]'s surface (Face[0] and Face[2] on one surface, split at Face[1]).
Here CV1 lies 0.1 mm off the wall's top: the cut on the wall's top ran from
a point that is not on the block's top, and the faces' new edges joined
points of different surfaces. Without the shortcut, the general walk cut
the end on all three faces, the tangent edges prolonged along their own
curves past the vertex: a tangent edge is a line of contact of two
surfaces that do not cross there, the prolonged flat side ran outside both
curved faces, and the three cuts crossed each other.

- The shortcut is taken only where CV1 lies on Face[2]'s surface, within
  CV1's tolerance and Edge[0]'s (the tolerances the point and the edge
  already have; no new limit).
- After the faces round the vertex are found: a face Face[k] whose edges at
  the vertex, Edge[k] and Edge[k + 1], are both tangent to the faces beside
  it (ChFi3d::IsTangentFaces, G1) and neither crossed by the blend's
  surface within its range is not cut; Face[k - 1] and Face[k + 1] are
  taken as neighbours whose common boundary is the intersection of their
  surfaces (GeomInt_IntSS, as for a B-spline edge the blend does not cross),
  the line through the vertex that crosses the blend. Its parameter at the
  vertex is the nearest extremum (an ellipse of two cylinders also passes
  the other end's vertex, the farthest point), its curves on the faces are
  put into the faces' periods at the vertex, and they are not shifted by a
  projection of the vertex onto them (the 3D curve and its curves on the
  faces share its parameters). The middle face is left as it is.
- Elsewhere nothing changes. No catch is added.

Upstream: changes the ends of blends at vertices of more than three edges
where the pro15368 shortcut was taken with CV1 off Face[2]'s surface (an
invalid shape before), and where a face lies between two faces tangent to
it, neither of its edges crossed (the general walk gave crossing cuts).

Test: geometry.port.1830 (geometry/tests/port/p1830-end-cut-across-a-tangent-face.cpp),
the half-disc block against the half-cylinder wall rounded and chamfered
with 0.5, 1 and 3: one valid solid each, the added volume that of the
section cut by both cylinders continued within 1e-5; the wall's top rounded
with 3 only at its edges, the side plane crossed: as before.

